Yes, but if we're going to be as pedantic as all that, then the spoiler 
itself is wrong:

 From scrl-343.txt:
> identify
>   Always identified when read.

Not if it's already identified.

>   blessed
>     You can identify items from your inventory:
>       # items:  1    2    3    4   all
>       chance:  1/5  1/5  1/5  1/5  1/5
>     If your luck is positive, then 1 item becomes 2 items.

Unless you have fewer than two items left in your inventory to be 
identified. Applies to all the numbers in the "# items:" line in the 
above list.

>     "This is an identify scroll."
>   uncursed
>     You can identify items from your inventory:
>       # items:   1     2     3     4    all
>       chance:  21/25  1/25  1/25  1/25  1/25

Same as above: if you have fewer items than that to identify, then it 
will only identify as many as you have.

>     "This is an identify scroll."
>   cursed
>     You can identify one item from your inventory.

Unless you don't have an item left to identify.

>     "This is an identify scroll."
>   confused
>     You merely identify the scroll.

Unless you've already identified scrolls of identify, in which case you 
identify nothing at all.

>     "You identify this as an identify scroll."

A redundant message in the above case.


Personally, I prefer to use words in the way that they are normally 
used, not in the manner dictated by rules of abstract logic. In other 
words, while it may be "true" to say that "If 0=1, then the moon is made 
of green cheese," but it is quite unhelpful to the person who actually 
wants to know what the moon is made of.

